1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,660
Aarav has a circuit with a
battery and two resistors.

2
00:00:03,660 --> 00:00:06,402
He wants to test five
different loads on it.

3
00:00:06,752 --> 00:00:09,794
Must he solve the whole
circuit again, five times?

4
00:00:10,144 --> 00:00:11,537
There is a shortcut.

5
00:00:11,537 --> 00:00:15,137
Squeeze the whole circuit into
one battery and one resistor.

6
00:00:15,637 --> 00:00:18,605
Shortcuts like this are
called network theorems.

7
00:00:18,955 --> 00:00:23,065
Superposition says, when there are
many sources, find the effect of each

8
00:00:23,065 --> 00:00:24,963
one alone, then add them up.

9
00:00:25,313 --> 00:00:29,808
Norton's theorem turns a circuit into
one current source, with one resistor

10
00:00:29,808 --> 00:00:30,762
in parallel.

11
00:00:31,112 --> 00:00:35,292
Maximum power transfer tells you
which load gets the most power.

12
00:00:35,642 --> 00:00:38,451
And the most used of all
is Thevenin's theorem.

13
00:00:39,101 --> 00:00:43,140
It was first found by Hermann von
Helmholtz in eighteen fifty three.

14
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:47,262
Leon Thevenin, a French engineer,
published it again in eighteen eighty

15
00:00:47,262 --> 00:00:48,162
three.

16
00:00:48,176 --> 00:00:49,754
Here is what it says.

17
00:00:49,754 --> 00:00:54,477
Any linear circuit, seen from two
terminals A and B, acts like one voltage

18
00:00:54,477 --> 00:00:56,903
source in series with one resistance.

19
00:00:57,553 --> 00:00:59,935
We call them VTH and RTH.

20
00:00:59,935 --> 00:01:03,322
The Thevenin voltage, and
the Thevenin resistance.

21
00:01:03,972 --> 00:01:05,958
Let's find them in four steps.

22
00:01:05,958 --> 00:01:10,276
Our circuit is a twelve volt battery,
with six ohms and three ohms.

23
00:01:10,626 --> 00:01:11,526
Step one.

24
00:01:11,349 --> 00:01:14,805
Take the load out, and mark
the two terminals, A and B.

25
00:01:15,505 --> 00:01:16,405
Step two.

26
00:01:16,353 --> 00:01:20,233
Find the voltage across A and
B, with nothing connected.

27
00:01:20,233 --> 00:01:23,967
Three ohms out of nine gets
one third of twelve volts.

28
00:01:23,967 --> 00:01:25,850
So VTH is four volts.

29
00:01:26,550 --> 00:01:27,511
Step three.

30
00:01:27,511 --> 00:01:30,194
Replace the battery with a plain wire.

31
00:01:30,194 --> 00:01:32,198
Now look in from A and B.

32
00:01:32,198 --> 00:01:35,048
Six ohms and three ohms are in parallel.

33
00:01:35,048 --> 00:01:36,625
RTH is two ohms.

34
00:01:37,325 --> 00:01:38,225
Step four.

35
00:01:38,194 --> 00:01:41,592
Draw a four volt source,
in series with two ohms.

36
00:01:41,592 --> 00:01:43,290
Then put the load back.

37
00:01:43,990 --> 00:01:48,648
The load current is VTH,
divided by RTH plus RL.

38
00:01:49,298 --> 00:01:51,348
Now let's check it on the breadboard.

39
00:01:51,348 --> 00:01:54,766
The real circuit on the left,
the Thevenin copy on the right.

40
00:01:55,116 --> 00:01:57,299
Connect a two ohm load to both.

41
00:01:57,299 --> 00:01:59,669
Each one carries exactly one ampere.

42
00:02:00,019 --> 00:02:02,296
Now turn the knob, and change the load.

43
00:02:02,646 --> 00:02:05,743
The scope shows the load
voltage of both circuits.

44
00:02:05,743 --> 00:02:07,673
The two traces never separate.

45
00:02:07,673 --> 00:02:10,582
For the load, the two
circuits are the same.

46
00:02:11,232 --> 00:02:15,249
Thevenin also tells us how to
get the most power into a load.

47
00:02:15,599 --> 00:02:19,085
Plot the load power, as the
load resistance changes.

48
00:02:19,435 --> 00:02:22,353
Too small a load, and
little voltage reaches it.

49
00:02:22,353 --> 00:02:24,908
Too big a load, and little current flows.

50
00:02:25,258 --> 00:02:28,166
The peak comes when the load equals RTH.

51
00:02:28,166 --> 00:02:29,635
Here, at two ohms.

52
00:02:30,335 --> 00:02:35,351
Then the maximum power is VTH
squared, divided by four RTH.

53
00:02:35,351 --> 00:02:37,277
Four squared over eight.

54
00:02:37,277 --> 00:02:38,269
Two watts.

55
00:02:38,919 --> 00:02:43,208
From any two points, a circuit is
just one battery and one resistor.

56
00:02:44,008 --> 00:02:45,143
Worked example.

57
00:02:45,143 --> 00:02:49,842
A twenty four volt battery feeds four
ohms, then twelve ohms across A and

58
00:02:49,842 --> 00:02:50,742
B.

59
00:02:50,257 --> 00:02:52,769
Find the current in a six ohm load.

60
00:02:53,119 --> 00:02:56,752
VTH is twenty four, times
twelve over sixteen.

61
00:02:56,752 --> 00:02:57,967
Eighteen volts.

62
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:02,930
With the battery shorted, RTH
is four and twelve in parallel.

63
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:05,479
Forty eight over sixteen, three ohms.

64
00:03:06,179 --> 00:03:09,662
So the load current is
eighteen, over three plus six.

65
00:03:09,662 --> 00:03:10,601
Two amperes.

66
00:03:11,251 --> 00:03:12,714
Now, the exam trap.

67
00:03:13,064 --> 00:03:17,977
To find RTH, many students just take
the battery out, and leave a gap.

68
00:03:18,327 --> 00:03:19,227
Wrong.

69
00:03:18,923 --> 00:03:23,041
Replace every voltage source with
a short circuit, a plain wire.

70
00:03:23,041 --> 00:03:26,758
Replace every current source
with an open circuit, a gap.

71
00:03:27,408 --> 00:03:28,969
Where is this asked?

72
00:03:28,969 --> 00:03:33,611
In PUC board papers, to state the
theorem and find a load current.

73
00:03:33,611 --> 00:03:37,069
JEE Main asks when a load
gets the maximum power.

74
00:03:37,719 --> 00:03:38,619
Try this.

75
00:03:38,564 --> 00:03:43,240
A nine volt battery feeds three
ohms, then six ohms across A and B.

76
00:03:43,240 --> 00:03:48,235
Which load across AB takes the maximum
power, and how much power is it?

77
00:03:53,635 --> 00:03:54,535
A.

78
00:03:54,066 --> 00:03:56,769
Two ohms, and four point five watts.

79
00:03:57,119 --> 00:04:00,252
VTH is nine, times six over nine.

80
00:04:00,252 --> 00:04:01,284
Six volts.

81
00:04:01,634 --> 00:04:04,345
RTH is three and six in parallel.

82
00:04:04,345 --> 00:04:06,586
Eighteen over nine, two ohms.

83
00:04:06,936 --> 00:04:10,499
Maximum power needs a load equal to RTH.

84
00:04:10,499 --> 00:04:11,489
Two ohms.

85
00:04:11,839 --> 00:04:15,077
And the power is six
squared, over four times two.

86
00:04:15,077 --> 00:04:17,968
Thirty six over eight,
four point five watts.

87
00:04:18,618 --> 00:04:20,139
Screenshot this card.

88
00:04:23,339 --> 00:04:25,153
Next, the AC waveform.

89
00:04:25,153 --> 00:04:29,072
Frequency, period, phase and
amplitude, on a live scope.
